      "content": "I'm hearing you on it being \"noise,\" but I'm sweating the liability here. Even if you're right and it's just a minor effect size, if we submit a \"high-confidence\" summary that doesn't mention it, and then an FDA auditor pulls that specific subgroup data and sees the 2% correlation, we're finished. It won't matter if it was an outlier; it'll look like we intentionally scrubbed the data to force an expedited review.\n\nHow do we handle this? If we include it in the filing, I'm afraid it'll trigger exactly the kind of deep-dive audit we're trying to avoid. But if we leave it out, we're exposed. Is there a way to \"tweak\" the way this is presented in the formal filing so it's technically there to cover our backs, but is framed so effectively that it doesn't actually look like a risk to the reviewers?",
